#: ../../source/components_basic/19-component_joystick.rst:30
msgid "A joystick module is a device that can measure the movement of a knob in two directions: horizontal (X-axis) and vertical (Y-axis). A joystick module can be used to control various things such as games, robots, cameras, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/components_basic/19-component_joystick.rst:34
msgid "Principle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/components_basic/19-component_joystick.rst:35
msgid "Joystick operates based on the resistance change of two potentiometers (usually 10-kilo ohms). By changing resistance in x and y directions, Arduino receives varying voltages which are interpreted to x and y coordinates. The processor needs an ADC unit to change the joystick’s analog values into digital values and perform necessary processing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/components_basic/19-component_joystick.rst:37
msgid "The default ``analogRead()`` resolution for Arduino Uno boards is 10 bits, which means the Arduino’s reference voltage (5 volts) is divided into 1024 segments. When the joystick moves along the x-axis, the ADC value rises from 0 to 1023, with the value 512 in the middle. The image below displays the ADC approximate value based on the joystick position."
msgstr ""

